I ran `crimes context src/Card.tsx` and read the file plus `tailwind.config.ts`. One note up front: this is an eval fixture (`evals/fixtures/07-stress-frontend`) and is deliberately messy scanner input, so I'm proposing the refactor rather than applying it — editing it would change what the scanner reports about itself.

## What `context` added beyond the scan

```
risk: MEDIUM (3 findings: 0 high, 1 medium, 2 low)
churn: 2 commits over 90d · last touched 3 months ago · 1 author
test gap: top-quartile
blast: 0 direct importers · no test file matched
```

That combination sets the strategy: **nothing imports `Card.tsx` and nothing tests it.** The refactor is safe to make aggressively — including changing the component's props — because there are no call sites to break. But nothing will catch a regression either, so the accessibility fix should land with a test rather than after one.

`context` also reported "findings on this file did not match any keyed guidance line" — there's no pre-written playbook here, the three fixes below are derived from the evidence.

## The actual source of the three findings

All three trace to a single inline `style={{...}}` object on the outer element (lines 11–21), plus two smaller ones on lines 24 and 27. The detector attributes most evidence to "line 10" because that's where the JSX element opens; the literals themselves are lines 12–20.

---

### 1. `accessible_interaction_risk` — medium, confidence 0.85
`fingerprint=accessible_interaction_risk::src/Card.tsx::`

Line 22: `onClick` on a `<div>` with no `role`, `tabIndex`, or `aria-label`. Keyboard users can't focus it, screen readers don't announce it as actionable, and Enter/Space do nothing.

The finding's `suggested_actions` offers two paths — switch to `<button>`, or add `role="button"` + `aria-label` + `tabIndex={0}`. **Take the first, but not literally.** Wrapping the whole card in a `<button>` is invalid here: `children` is an unconstrained `React.ReactNode`, so a caller passing a link or button produces nested interactive elements — which is a *worse* accessibility bug than the one being fixed, and one the detector won't flag.

The right shape is to stop making the container clickable at all and give the card an explicit action:

```tsx
type CardProps = {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  /** Accessible name for the card's action; required when onActivate is set. */
  label: string;
  onActivate?: () => void;
  sidebar?: React.ReactNode;
};

export function Card({ children, label, onActivate, sidebar }: CardProps) {
  return (
    <section className={styles.card} aria-label={label}>
      <div className={styles.layout}>
        <div className={styles.body}>{children}</div>
        {sidebar ? <aside className={styles.sidebar}>{sidebar}</aside> : null}
      </div>
      {onActivate ? (
        <button type="button" className={styles.action} onClick={onActivate}>
          {label}
        </button>
      ) : null}
    </section>
  );
}
```

Real `<button>` semantics means no ARIA at all — focus, Enter/Space, and the a11y tree come free. If whole-card click is a genuine product requirement, use the stretched-link pattern (a real `<button>`/`<a>` in the card with an `::after` overlay) rather than reinstating `role="button"` on the container.

The minimum-diff fallback — `role="button" tabIndex={0}` plus an `onKeyDown` handling Enter and Space — clears the detector but keeps the nested-interactive hazard. Only reach for it if the props can't change; here they can, since nothing imports this file.

### 2. `design_token_escape` — low, confidence 0.75
`fingerprint=design_token_escape::src/Card.tsx::`

9 raw values across 4 kinds: `#ff00aa`, `#0a0a0a`, `#cccccc`, `#1a73e8`, `rgb(0, 0, 0)`, `#333333`, `#666666`, plus px spacing and the shadow.

The finding's fix shape is "replace raw values with the design token" — but **the tokens don't exist yet.** `tailwind.config.ts` defines exactly three colors (`brand #0050ff`, `surface #f7f7fa`, `ink #111111`), and *none* of the nine escaped values matches any of them. This isn't a 1:1 swap; the escape is hiding a gap in the token scale. That's the part an agent extending this file would copy forward.

Two things fall out of that:

- `#ff00aa` (hot magenta) as a card background against `#0a0a0a` text is almost certainly placeholder or debug styling, not a design decision. Don't launder it into a token — replace it with `surface`/`ink` and confirm.
- `#cccccc`, `#333333`, `#666666`, `#1a73e8` (Google blue) are a border, two muted text greys, and a focus ring. Those are four real token slots the design system is missing: `--color-border`, `--color-muted`, `--color-muted-strong`, `--color-focus`. Adding them is the fix; the component change is downstream of it.

Move presentation into a stylesheet consuming tokens, so the JSX carries no values at all:

```css
/* tokens.css — the three from tailwind.config.ts, plus the four the escape revealed */
:root {
  --color-brand:  #0050ff;
  --color-surface: #f7f7fa;
  --color-ink:     #111111;

  --color-border:       /* was #cccccc — needs design sign-off */;
  --color-muted:        /* was #333333 */;
  --color-muted-strong: /* was #666666 */;
  --color-focus:        /* was #1a73e8 */;

  --space-6:  1.5rem;
  --radius-md: 0.5rem;
  --shadow-sm: 0 2px 4px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.2);
}
```

```css
/* Card.module.css */
.card {
  container-type: inline-size;
  background: var(--color-surface);
  color: var(--color-ink);
  border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);
  padding: var(--space-6);
}
.action:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid var(--color-focus);
  outline-offset: 2px;
}
```

One honest caveat: moving values into a `.css` file also moves them out of `language-js`'s view — coverage shows only the two `.js`-family files are parsed. The finding would clear either way. The *token indirection* is what actually fixes it; the file move alone would be gaming the detector.

If Tailwind is genuinely wired into the build (the fixture has a config but no dependency), the same fix is `className="bg-surface text-ink border-border"` after extending the theme — the token-gap work is identical.

### 3. `responsive_fragility` — low, confidence 0.65
`fingerprint=responsive_fragility::src/Card.tsx::`

`width: 800` (line 12), `width: 720` (line 24), `fontSize: 18` (line 24), and no `@media` anywhere. An 800px fixed width overflows every phone viewport; the 720px child forces horizontal scroll inside it.

Note the fix shape here deliberately does *not* say "add media queries" — it says drive layout from container queries or a fluid scale. That matters for a card: a card's correct layout depends on the space *it* was given, not the viewport. A media query gets it wrong the moment the card is placed in a sidebar.

```css
.card { container-type: inline-size; max-width: 50rem; }

.layout {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 3fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
  gap: var(--space-6);
}

.body { font-size: clamp(1rem, 0.95rem + 0.4cqi, 1.125rem); }
.sidebar { font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--color-muted-strong); }

/* collapse to one column when the card itself is narrow */
@container (max-width: 40rem) {
  .layout { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
}
```

`width: 800` → `max-width: 50rem` with the grid handling intrinsic width; the 720/240 split becomes the `3fr / 1fr` ratio; `fontSize: 18` becomes a `clamp()` scaling on container inline size. Also drop `height: 600` — a fixed height on a component with arbitrary `children` clips content, and it's a latent bug the detector didn't flag.

---

## Sequencing

1. **Tokens first** (`design_token_escape`) — the four missing colors need a decision before anything consumes them. This is the only step with an external dependency.
2. **Structure + a11y** (`accessible_interaction_risk`) — the prop change is free right now: 0 direct importers. It gets expensive the moment someone imports this.
3. **Layout** (`responsive_fragility`) — falls out of the grid once the inline styles are gone.
4. **Add `Card.test.tsx`** — `context` found no test file by any convention and scored `test_gap: 1.0`. Minimum coverage: the action is reachable by Tab, fires on Enter and Space, and has an accessible name.

Verify with `crimes scan` from the fixture root — all three fingerprints should clear. Given the caveat in §2, check that the token file genuinely has no unexplained raw values rather than trusting the count going to zero.
